# Entity details design

## Usage (caller's view)

A search result's name is an ordinary link to `/members/4514?q=smith&strictness=1` or `/funders/<uuid>?q=smith&strictness=1`. An MP page displays the latest 20 declarations with their funding entries. A funder page displays declared support by recipients' latest recorded party, followed by the top ten recipients within each currency. Every funder page states that parties reflect the latest stored membership, values describe declared support rather than necessarily direct donations, and exact source names define funder identity.

```rust
// Member HTTP handler, after parsing the path into MemberId.
let details = state.entity_details_service.member(member_id).await?;
Ok(ApiSuccess::new(StatusCode::OK, MemberDetailsResponse::from(details)))

// Funder HTTP handler, after parsing the path into FunderId.
let details = state.entity_details_service.funder(funder_id).await?;
Ok(ApiSuccess::new(StatusCode::OK, FunderDetailsResponse::from(details)))
```

```svelte
<!-- Indicator button remains outside the name anchor. -->
<EntityIndicator {entity} />
<a href={entityHref(entity, searchParams)}>{entity.name}</a>
<!-- A recipient on a funder page links to the member page in the same way. -->
```

## Problem

Search currently discards stable identifiers at the HTTP boundary, and `Funder` does not retain its database UUID. The source schema permits repeated funding occurrences, missing values, and declarations without funding entries. The new read path must preserve those facts while keeping aggregation exact and keeping existing search behavior intact. Existing domain services, repository traits, SQLx macros, and explicit errors establish the integration pattern.

## Shape

Use a dedicated details service with two methods and one dedicated read repository. The repository loads bounded member declarations and funder totals grouped by recipient and currency. The service derives party totals and recipient rankings from the same recipient aggregates. No raw payment collection crosses the repository boundary for funder pages, and there is one source for both displayed summaries.

```rust
// domain/models/funder.rs: preserve the existing model, add persistent identity.
pub struct FunderId(Uuid); // FromStr validates at the HTTP boundary.
pub struct Funder { id: FunderId, name: String, funder_kind: FunderKind }
// Update constructors and all current callers. Never fabricate identity.
// Reuse MemberId; add its checked path parser and accessor rather than a second ID.

// domain/models/entity_details.rs: fields shown for design; implement existing getter style.
pub struct MemberDetails {
    member: MemberProfile,
    declarations: Vec<RecentDeclaration>,
    declaration_limit: usize,
}
pub struct MemberProfile { id: MemberId, name: String, party: RecordedParty, is_current_commons: bool }
pub struct RecordedParty { id: PartyId, name: String }
pub struct RecentDeclaration {
    source_id: i64, category_name: String,
    registered_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>, entries: Vec<DeclaredFunding>,
}
pub struct DeclaredFunding {
    funder: Option<FunderReference>, amount: Option<BigDecimal>,
    currency: Option<String>, payment_type: Option<String>,
}
pub struct FunderReference { id: FunderId, name: String }
pub struct FunderProfile { funder: Funder, company_number: Option<String> }
pub struct FunderDetails {
    profile: FunderProfile, currencies: Vec<CurrencyBreakdown>,
    entry_count: u64, unvalued_entry_count: u64,
}
pub struct CurrencyBreakdown {
    currency: String, parties: Vec<PartyTotal>, top_recipients: Vec<RecipientTotal>,
}
pub struct PartyTotal { party: RecordedParty, amount: BigDecimal }
pub struct RecipientTotal { member: MemberProfile, amount: BigDecimal }
// Read repository result, not an HTTP DTO. One row per recipient/currency.
pub struct FunderFunding { profile: FunderProfile, allocations: Vec<RecipientAllocation> }
pub struct RecipientAllocation {
    member: MemberProfile, currency: Option<String>, known_total: Option<BigDecimal>,
    entry_count: u64, unvalued_entry_count: u64,
}

// domain/repositories/entity_details.rs
pub trait EntityDetailsRepo: Clone + Send + Sync + 'static {
    fn member(&self, id: MemberId, recent_limit: usize)
        -> impl Future<Output = Result<Option<MemberDetails>, EntityDetailsRepoError>> + Send;
    fn funder_funding(&self, id: FunderId)
        -> impl Future<Output = Result<Option<FunderFunding>, EntityDetailsRepoError>> + Send;
}
// domain/services/entity_details.rs, interface/error submodules as existing precedent.
pub trait EntityDetailsService: Clone + Send + Sync + 'static {
    fn member(&self, id: MemberId)
        -> impl Future<Output = Result<MemberDetails, EntityDetailsError>> + Send;
    fn funder(&self, id: FunderId)
        -> impl Future<Output = Result<FunderDetails, EntityDetailsError>> + Send;
}
pub struct Service<R> { repo: R }
fn summarize_funder(funding: FunderFunding) -> FunderDetails { todo!() }
pub enum EntityDetailsError { NotFound, UnexpectedError(String) }
```

`BigDecimal` is a direct domain dependency, matched to SQLx's version rather than imported from `sqlx::types`. SQLx already enables its decimal support. HTTP DTOs serialize decimal values as strings; browser code never converts them to JavaScript numbers. Format grouping with string operations, retain exact fractional digits, and show the source currency text.

`outbound/entity_details.rs` owns SQL and database-row conversion. Member retrieval selects 20 declaration IDs before joining funding entries. Order by registration date descending with nulls last, then source ID descending; order entries by their UUID for stability. Left joins retain declarations without entries and entries without a funder. A missing entry UUID means no entry; null amount never means no entry. Read member existence and details in one SQL statement or one read transaction with a consistent snapshot.

Funder retrieval joins only its own funding occurrences to declarations and members, then groups by recipient ID and source currency. `SUM(amount)` uses every occurrence, never `DISTINCT`. A known total exists only when currency is non-null and at least one amount is known. Unknown currencies never produce a sum because they may represent different currencies. Count an entry as unvalued once when amount or currency is missing. Keep the existing funder row even with no entries, so absence is different from an empty result. No new migration or index is required.

The service owns the 20-declaration and ten-recipient limits, not client options. Its pure funder summarizer groups known currency totals by recorded party ID and ranks recipients independently within each currency by decimal descending, name, then member ID. It adds occurrence and unvalued counts over all rows before truncation. Render no ranking for unknown currencies and explain the unvalued count. Negative and zero reported amounts remain reported amounts. This avoids invented conversions and retains source meaning.

The service hides summary construction and not-found policy behind two calls. The repository hides relational joins and aggregation behind domain results. HTTP only parses IDs and maps domain outcomes to 200, 404, invalid-request, or the existing sanitized 500 response. This keeps the service useful rather than a forwarding wrapper, per boundary-discipline and minimize-reader-load.

`AppState<E, D>` adds the details service; `server.rs` wires it alongside search. `http/handlers/entity_details.rs` owns the new response DTOs. Routes are `/members/{id}` and `/funders/{id}`. Search preserves its existing fields and adds an `id` string, interpreted together with `kind`; the frontend parser produces a discriminated MP/Funder union. Funder repository search selects its UUID and every model constructor receives it.

Frontend ownership is small: `App.svelte` selects the route from `location.pathname`; the existing search screen moves intact into `SearchPage.svelte`; `EntityDetails.svelte` owns request state and layout; `entity-details.ts` owns URL parsing, typed fetches, response parsing, and exact decimal display. Use ordinary full-document links instead of introducing a client router. Browser Back, reload, modifier-click, and new tabs then work natively. Copy search parameters into detail links and derive the explicit Back to search URL from them. Require the host's existing SPA fallback for direct paths; verify it in the running app.

Details use a discriminated state of loading, ready member, ready funder, not found, and error. Abort on teardown and ignore obsolete request completion. Retry starts a new request. A 200 result with empty declarations or currency groups is a ready state with clear empty copy. MP pages have party/member identity and a dated declaration list. Funders have legal-kind identity, company number when available, and tabular currency summaries. Preserve dark Manrope styling; use labels and layout as well as distinct accent colors. Keep all tooltip buttons outside anchors.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- We accept full-document detail navigation for native browser semantics and no routing dependency.
- We accept transferring every recipient/currency aggregate for one funder so party totals and rankings share one input. The transfer is bounded by recipients and currencies, not payment history.
- We accept a fixed recent-declaration window and fixed top-ten ranking for this feature. The interface labels these limits rather than implying completeness.

## Alternatives considered

- Separate repository calls for party totals and top recipients hide more SQL but duplicate grouping rules and can disagree when records change between calls. The service would become a pass-through layer with little ownership.
- Load every funding occurrence and aggregate in the service. This makes ownership simple but puts unbounded historical data into application memory. Recipient/currency aggregation keeps the same public capability with far less transfer.
- A client router with a generic polymorphic entity endpoint saves document reloads but adds navigation state and transport dispatch beyond the two required pages.

## Synthesis decision

Candidate only; the orchestrator selects the base and records adaptations.

## Implementation reconciliation

No implementation deviations yet. The scalar limits and precise response field names can be reconciled without changing ownership or outcomes.

## Open questions and risks

Can production serve the frontend entry point on direct member and funder paths? Verify this before shipping; if it cannot, use query-based detail URLs with the same native navigation policy.

## Next implementation step

Add persistent funder identity and the dedicated details repository, then prove aggregation against fixtures containing duplicate values, multiple currencies, missing amounts/currencies, empty declarations, unknown funders, exact decimals, and date ties before wiring the service and pages.
